# ArticleCard

A full-bleed article preview for listings and related-article sections, not for use within article content. In a static `.html` file, copy the markup for each card and edit its attributes and text directly. No imports or JavaScript are required.

## Inputs

| Input | Type | Required / default | HTML mapping |
|---|---|---|---|
| `href` | string | Required | Article URL in the card link’s `href` |
| `title` | string | Required | Article title in the heading |
| `image` | string | Required | Full-bleed image URL in `img src` |
| `description` | string | Optional | Excerpt paragraph |
| `imageAlt` | string | Article title | Image `alt`; prefer text that describes the image |
| `date` | string | Optional | Formatted text inside `<time>` |
| `datetime` | string | Optional | ISO value in `<time datetime>`; supply it when showing a date |
| `author` | name and avatar URL | Optional | Author name and avatar image |
| `ctaText` | string | `Read more` | Text in an optional CTA span, not a button |
| `className` | string | Optional | Extra classes on `<article>` |
| `variant` | `default` or `compact` | `default` | Add `article-card--compact` for the compact size |

**Semantic hints:** `card`, `article-card`, `preview`, `teaser`, `post-card`, `blog-card`, `article-preview`, `content-card`

## Structure and accessibility

Use one link around the image, overlay, and bottom-aligned content. Omit unused optional elements. Match the heading level to the page outline. Give the cover meaningful alt text and use `alt=""` for an avatar beside the author’s name. Keep text readable over bright images and provide a visible keyboard focus outline.

## Complete example

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Article preview</title>
<style>
  .article-card { max-width: 40rem; }
  .article-card--compact { max-width: 26rem; }
  .article-card a {
    position: relative; isolation: isolate; display: flex;
    align-items: end; min-height: 12rem; aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    overflow: hidden; background: #222; color: #fff;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .article-card .cover, .article-card a::before {
    position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%;
  }
  .article-card .cover { z-index: -2; object-fit: cover; }
  .article-card a::before {
    content: ""; z-index: -1;
    background: linear-gradient(to top, #000 0%, #000c 55%, transparent);
  }
  .article-card .copy { padding: 1.25rem; background: #0009; }
  .article-card--compact .copy { padding: .75rem; }
  .article-card h2 { margin: .25rem 0; }
  .article-card a:hover { box-shadow: 0 4px 12px #0005; }
  .article-card a:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #175cd3; outline-offset: 4px; }
</style>
<article class="article-card">
  <a href="/articles/machine-learning-basics">
    <img class="cover" src="/images/ml-basics.jpg"
         alt="Laptop displaying a scatter plot">
    <div class="copy">
      <time datetime="2024-01-15">January 15, 2024</time>
      <h2>Getting Started with Machine Learning</h2>
      <p><img src="/avatars/jane.jpg" alt="" width="24" height="24"> Jane Smith</p>
      <span>Read more</span>
    </div>
  </a>
</article>
</html>
```

framework_component_specs.HeroSection:
# HeroSection

## Purpose
A reusable HTML pattern for prominent page headings on listing pages, category pages, and other landing pages. For individual articles, use the article-specific header pattern instead.

## Configuration
Copy the markup into a `.html` file. No imports, JavaScript, or runtime props are required.

| Original prop | HTML equivalent | Required / Default |
|---|---|---|
| `title` (`string`) | Main heading text in `<h1>` | Required |
| `description` (`string`) | Subtitle or summary in `<p class="hero__description">` | Optional; omit the element if unused |
| `children` (`ReactNode`) | Additional HTML, such as a search form or CTA links, inside `<div class="hero__content">` | Optional; omit the wrapper if unused |
| `className` (`string`) | Extra CSS classes appended to the hero’s `class` attribute | Optional; none |
| `variant` (`primary`, `secondary`, `neutral`) | Use `hero` alone for `primary`, or add `hero--secondary` or `hero--neutral` | Optional; `primary` |

## Semantic Hints
`hero`, `header`, `banner`, `page-header`, `page-hero`, `headline`, `masthead`, `jumbotron`

## Structure and Styling
- Use a full-width `<section>` with a centered, width-constrained inner container.
- Use a large, bold heading, a muted description with comfortable line height, and top spacing before optional content.
- Use responsive heading sizes and vertical padding. Primary uses the brand background; secondary uses a lighter brand tint; neutral uses gray or white.

## Accessibility
- Use the hero title as the page’s main `<h1>`. Name the section with `aria-labelledby` pointing to the heading’s unique `id`.
- Maintain sufficient text and control contrast. Use native links, buttons, and form controls with visible keyboard focus. Give search inputs a label rather than relying on a placeholder.

## Complete Example: `index.html`

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>Articles</title>
  <style>
    body { margin: 0; font: 1rem/1.5 system-ui, sans-serif; }
    .hero {
      padding: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 4rem) 1rem;
      background: #183a70;
      color: #fff;
      --hero-muted: #dbe8ff;
    }
    .hero--secondary {
      background: #e5efff;
      color: #183a70;
      --hero-muted: #384e6e;
    }
    .hero--neutral {
      background: #f3f4f6;
      color: #1f2937;
      --hero-muted: #4b5563;
    }
    .hero__inner { max-width: 72rem; margin: 0 auto; }
    .hero h1 { margin: 0; font-size: clamp(1.875rem, 5vw, 3rem); }
    .hero__description {
      max-width: 60ch;
      margin: .75rem 0 0;
      color: var(--hero-muted);
    }
    .hero__content { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
    .hero a { color: inherit; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <main>
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
      <div class="hero__inner">
        <h1 id="hero-title">Articles</h1>
        <p class="hero__description">Browse our latest articles and resources.</p>
        <div class="hero__content">
          <a href="#latest">Jump to latest articles</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <h2 id="latest">Latest articles</h2>
  </main>
</body>
</html>
```

