# ArticleEnhancer (.html)

Enhances existing article markup with a table of contents (TOC) and Schema.org JSON-LD. Place the TOC placeholder outside the content being scanned, typically in the article layout. The article remains readable without JavaScript.

## Configuration

| Option | Type | Default | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| `articleTitle` | string | Required | Name for HowTo data |
| `contentSelector` | string | Required | CSS selector for the content container |
| `articleDescription` | string | — | Optional HowTo description |
| `enableToc` | boolean | `true` | Generate a TOC |
| `enableHowTo` | boolean | `true` | Generate HowTo data for a marked step list |
| `enableMediaObject` | boolean | `true` | Generate MediaObject data for images |
| `children` | HTML markup | — | Author directly inside the content container |

**Semantic hints:** `enhancer`, `wrapper`, `article-wrapper`, `enhancement`, `seo`, `structured-data`, `meta`

## Complete example

The inline script runs after the article markup. It preserves existing heading IDs, assigns unique IDs where needed, and nests `h3` links under the preceding `h2`. A debounced observer refreshes the TOC and replaces the enhancer’s JSON-LD in `<head>` when content changes. Mark only genuine instructional lists with `data-howto`.

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>Plant a seed</title>
</head>
<body>
<main>
  <h1>Plant a seed</h1>
  <nav id="toc" aria-label="On this page" hidden></nav>
  <article id="content">
    <h2>Directions 🌱</h2>
    <h3>Planting</h3>
    <ol data-howto>
      <li>Fill a pot with soil.</li>
      <li>Plant the seed and water it.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>
</main>
<script>
const {
  articleTitle, contentSelector, articleDescription,
  enableToc = true, enableHowTo = true, enableMediaObject = true
} = { articleTitle: 'Plant a seed', contentSelector: '#content' };

const article = document.querySelector(contentSelector);
const toc = document.querySelector('#toc');
const schema = document.createElement('script');
schema.type = 'application/ld+json';

function refresh() {
  if (enableToc) {
    const list = document.createElement('ul');
    let parent, sub;
    let n = 0;
    for (const h of article.querySelectorAll('h2,h3')) {
      if (!h.id) {
        let id;
        do { id = `section-${++n}`; } while (document.getElementById(id));
        h.id = id;
      }
      const li = document.createElement('li');
      const a = document.createElement('a');
      a.href = '#' + encodeURIComponent(h.id);
      a.textContent = h.textContent.replace(/\p{Emoji_Presentation}|\p{Extended_Pictographic}|[\uFE0F\u200D\u20E3]/gu, '').trim() || h.id;
      li.append(a);
      if (h.matches('h2')) {
        parent = li;
        sub = null;
      }
      if (h.matches('h3') && parent) {
        if (!sub) parent.append(sub = document.createElement('ul'));
        sub.append(li);
      } else list.append(li);
    }
    toc.replaceChildren(list);
    toc.hidden = !list.children.length;
  }
  const graph = [];
  const steps = [...article.querySelectorAll('ol[data-howto] > li')]
    .map(li => li.textContent.trim()).filter(Boolean);
  if (enableHowTo && steps.length) graph.push({
    '@type': 'HowTo', name: articleTitle, description: articleDescription,
    step: steps.map(text => ({ '@type': 'HowToStep', text }))
  });
  if (enableMediaObject) for (const img of article.querySelectorAll('img[src]')) {
    graph.push({ '@type': 'MediaObject', contentUrl: img.src });
  }
  schema.textContent = JSON.stringify({ '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@graph': graph });
  if (graph.length) document.head.append(schema);
  else schema.remove();
}

refresh();
let timer;
new MutationObserver(() => {
  clearTimeout(timer);
  timer = setTimeout(refresh, 100);
}).observe(article, {
  subtree: true, childList: true, characterData: true,
  attributeFilter: ['id', 'src', 'data-howto']
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

framework_component_specs.TableOfContents:
# TableOfContents

A collapsible article outline in a `.html` page. Show only `h2` links, strip emoji from link labels, and group `h3` headings under their preceding `h2` internally. Place it in the article’s TOC slot, typically populated when the page is generated.

## Configuration

No imports or framework props are needed. Configure the component through HTML and optional inline JavaScript.

| Setting | Type | Default | Static HTML equivalent |
|---|---|---|---|
| `items` | `TocItem[]` | Required | Build `<li><a href="#id">label</a></li>` entries for level-2 items. |
| `title` | string | `Contents` | Text inside `<summary>`. |
| `ariaLabel` | string | `Table of contents` | `aria-label` on `<nav>`. |
| `className` | string | — | Extra classes on `<details>`. |
| `activeId` | string | — | ID of the section whose link has `aria-current="true"`. |
| `onItemClick` | function receiving the ID | — | Optional delegated `click` listener on `<nav>`; read the link’s fragment. |

A `TocItem` has an `id` matching a unique heading ID, a plain-text `label` with emoji removed, a `level` of `2` or `3`, and optional `children` containing the parent’s level-3 items. Do not render the children in the TOC.

**Semantic hints:** `toc`, `table-of-contents`, `navigation`, `outline`, `contents`, `nav`, `page-nav`, `section-nav`, `sidebar-toc`

## Behavior and styling

Use native links and `<details>` for keyboard support. Collapse after link activation and focus the destination heading. Track scroll position with `IntersectionObserver`, mark only one link current, and announce changes through a polite status region. Keep the outline compact, with a muted title, indigo links, and a bold active link. Respect reduced-motion preferences.

## Complete example

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Article outline</title>
<style>
  .toc { max-width: 16rem; }
  summary { color: #555; font-size: .9rem; cursor: pointer; }
  .toc a { color: #4338ca; text-decoration: none; }
  .toc a:hover, .toc a:focus-visible { text-decoration: underline; }
  [aria-current="true"] { font-weight: bold; }
  #status { position: absolute; clip-path: inset(50%); }
  section { min-height: 70vh; }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    html { scroll-behavior: smooth; }
  }
</style>
<details class="toc" open>
  <summary>Contents</summary>
  <nav aria-label="Table of contents">
    <ul>
      <li><a href="#intro">Introduction</a></li>
      <li><a href="#setup">Setup</a></li>
    </ul>
  </nav>
</details>
<p id="status" role="status"></p>
<article>
  <section><h2 id="intro" tabindex="-1">👋 Introduction</h2></section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="setup" tabindex="-1">Setup</h2>
    <h3 id="requirements">Requirements</h3>
  </section>
</article>
<script>
  const toc = document.querySelector('.toc');
  const links = [...toc.querySelectorAll('a')];
  const heads = links.map(a => document.querySelector(a.hash));
  let active;
  const observer = new IntersectionObserver(() => {
    const next = heads.filter(h => h.getBoundingClientRect().top <=
      innerHeight / 4).pop() || heads[0];
    if (next === active) return;
    active = next;
    links.forEach(a => a.hash === '#' + next.id
      ? a.setAttribute('aria-current', 'true')
      : a.removeAttribute('aria-current'));
    document.querySelector('#status').textContent =
      links[heads.indexOf(next)].textContent;
  }, { rootMargin: '0px 0px -75% 0px' });
  heads.forEach(h => observer.observe(h));
  links.forEach((a, i) => a.onclick = () => {
    heads[i].focus({ preventScroll: true });
    toc.open = false;
  });
</script>
```

framework_component_specs.ArticleCardBySlug:
# ArticleCardBySlug

## Purpose
Render an article preview from its slug. In a static `.html` file, use an `<article>` with `data-slug` and a shared inline script to resolve metadata. No imports or framework are required.

## Inputs

| Input | HTML | Type | Default | Description |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `slug` (required) | `data-slug` | string | — | Key in the article registry. |
| `ctaText` | `data-cta-text` | string | `Read more` | Call-to-action link text. |
| `className` | `class` | string | — | Append extra classes after `article-card`. |
| `descriptionOverride` | `data-description-override` | string | — | Replaces the registry description. |
| `variant` | `data-variant` | `default` or `compact` | `default` | Selects the card layout. |
| `authorOverride` | `data-author-override` | `Author` | — | ID of the replacement author in the author registry. |

**Semantic hints:** `card`, `article-card`, `slug-card`, `dynamic-card`, `registry-card`, `auto-card`

## Usage and behavior
Use when you know an article’s slug and want to avoid duplicating its metadata. Repeat the `<article>` elements inside any grid or list. The script fills a shared card template with the title, description, image, formatted publication date, resolved author, and article link. It supplies a fallback image when none is set. For an unknown slug, it logs a warning and removes the empty card.

Use shared `.article-card` styles; target `[data-variant="compact"]` for the compact layout. No wrapper-specific styles are needed.

## Complete `.html` example

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>Article cards</title>

<article class="article-card" data-slug="machine-learning-basics"></article>
<article class="article-card featured" data-slug="machine-learning-basics"
  data-variant="compact" data-description-override="Start here."
  data-author-override="lee" data-cta-text="Read the guide"></article>

<template id="article-card">
  <img alt="" width="320" height="160">
  <h2></h2>
  <p></p>
  <footer><time></time> · <span></span></footer>
  <a></a>
</template>

<script>
  const articles = new Map([["machine-learning-basics", {
    title: "Machine Learning Basics",
    description: "Learn how models find patterns in data.",
    date: "2025-02-12", author: "sam", url: "/articles/machine-learning-basics.html"
  }]]);
  const authors = { sam: "Sam Rivera", lee: "Lee Chen" };
  const fallback = "data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 2 1'%3E%3Cpath fill='%23ddd' d='M0 0h2v1H0z'/%3E%3C/svg%3E";
  const format = new Intl.DateTimeFormat("en", {
    dateStyle: "medium", timeZone: "UTC"
  });

  for (const card of document.querySelectorAll(".article-card[data-slug]")) {
    const d = card.dataset;
    const a = articles.get(d.slug);
    if (!a) {
      console.warn("Unknown article:", d.slug);
      card.remove();
      continue;
    }
    card.append(document.getElementById("article-card").content.cloneNode(true));
    const get = selector => card.querySelector(selector);
    get("img").src = a.image || fallback;
    get("h2").textContent = a.title;
    get("p").textContent = d.descriptionOverride ?? a.description;
    get("time").dateTime = a.date;
    get("time").textContent = format.format(new Date(a.date));
    get("span").textContent = authors[d.authorOverride ?? a.author] ?? "Unknown author";
    get("a").href = a.url;
    get("a").textContent = d.ctaText ?? "Read more";
  }
</script>
```

